{"name":"Hugo","hex":"#e6cfcc","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Hugo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A male given name from the Germanic languages, a Latinized form of Hugh.","A surname from French."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Hugo Award, a statuette given out by the World Science Fiction Society."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Hugo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hugo (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Hugo is a 2011 American adventure drama film directed and produced by Martin Scorsese, and adapted for the screen by John Logan. Based on Brian Selznick's 2007 book The Invention of Hugo Cabret, it tells the story of a boy who lives alone in the Gare Montparnasse railway station in Paris in the 1930s, only to become embroiled in a mystery surrounding his late father's automaton and the pioneering filmmaker Georges Méliès."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/73/Hugo_Poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugo_(film)","wikidata":"Q206576"},{"title":"Hugo Award","blocks":[{"text":"The Hugo Award is an annual literary award for the best science fiction or fantasy works and achievements of the previous year, given at the World Science Fiction Convention (Worldcon) and chosen by its members. The award is administered by the World Science Fiction Society. It is named after Hugo Gernsback, the founder of the pioneering science fiction magazine Amazing Stories. Hugos were first given in 1953, at the 11th World Science Fiction Convention, and have been awarded every year since 1955. In 2010, Wired called the Hugo \"the premier award in the science fiction genre\", while The Guardian has called it the most important science fiction award alongside the Nebula Award."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Hugo_Award_Logo.svg/330px-Hugo_Award_Logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_cam